    "openrouter_cost_usd": 0.00159185,
    "reply": "Cystic fibrosis is associated with NCBIGene:1080, which encodes the CF transmembrane conductance regulator [1]. The disease also shows connections to NCBIGene:2212 (Fc gamma receptor IIa) and NCBIGene:7040 (transforming growth factor beta 1) [3][4]. The condition itself is recorded as Cystic fibrosis [2].\n\n## Disease Context\n\nCystic fibrosis presents with multiple clinical features including asthma [11], bronchiectasis [15], clubbing of fingers [19], dehydration [23], diarrhea [27], hemoptysis [28], hepatomegaly [29], and hepatosplenomegaly [30]. These manifestations reflect the systemic nature of the disorder beyond its pulmonary hallmark.\n\n## Literature Evidence\n\nRecent publications address varied aspects of cystic fibrosis care and complications. Encephalopathy represents a rare complication of cystic fibrosis liver disease, with hyperammonemic encephalopathy precipitated by triggers such as systemic corticosteroids in previously stable patients [26: \"hyperammonemic encephalopathy\" can be precipitated in previously stable CFLD by various triggers including systemic corticosteroids\"]. Other publications cover nutrition [10], azithromycin use [18], and cystic fibrosis-related diabetes [22].\n\n## Clinical Research\n\nActive clinical investigation includes trials on quadriceps muscle electrostimulation [8] and pregnancy outcomes [24]. These studies reflect ongoing efforts to manage the multisystem burden of cystic fibrosis. The literature entity Cystic Fibrosis is indexed for retrieval [7], and the condition is catalogued in MedGen [6].",
    "dur": 13.283
   },
